```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Vector DB engineers and ANN researchers diagnosing per-item recall failures in greedy graph indexes (NSW/HNSW) during index build/tuning, especially when trying to improve tail recall without raising ef_search (latency).
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only in current form; modest effect (|rho|~0.15) on 4k synthetic 12-d GMM with pure-Python single-layer NSW at ef=k=10 (recall 0.822); production systems use multi-layer HNSW, larger real embeddings (SIFT1M, sentence corpora), higher ef, where hubness may strengthen the law but stability of blind-spot identity remains unproven (mean rank stability 0.134 < 0.30 threshold, 0.079 at denom>=5). No measured repair payoff yet.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Requires L1 validation on production indexes (faiss-HNSW/hnswlib) and real high-dim embeddings; blind-spot identity reshuffles with insertion order, blocking static per-item repair lists; overhead of tracking reverse-kNN in-degree at scale; existing mitigations (raise ef, tune M/ef_construction) already trade latency for recall; adding reverse edges risks memory/latency bloat without proven per-item lift at fixed ef]
RATIONALE: Partial Spearman(in-degree,miss|density)=-0.152 CI[-0.203,-0.097] confirms density-independent population law (EXP-0045 L0 RESULTS); stability across 5 seeds mean 0.134 < 0.30 threshold shows blind-spot set is build-dependent, not item-intrinsic (EXP-0045 Stability sub-check); prior art covers per-query reachability (2510.22316), aggregate recall vs order (2405.17813), and high-in-degree hubs for speed (2412.01940), leaving low-in-degree recall deficit as a distinct per-item, query-independent phenomenon (Prior-art delta); honest negative is actionable: density is not the confound (density<->miss -0.079, opposite sign) and static blind-spot lists are unreliable, directing effort toward population-level graph construction (e.g., min in-degree) rather than per-item patching (Honest reading).
```
